\section{Introduction}
%=============================================================================

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as a powerful paradigm for general-purpose robot manipulation. $\pi_0$ \citep{black2024pi0} demonstrated that a flow-matching architecture built on a pretrained vision-language model can master diverse manipulation tasks across multiple robot platforms, and its successor $\pi_{0.5}$ \citep{black2025pi05} extended this capability to open-world generalization through co-training on heterogeneous data. These models generate smooth, language-conditioned action trajectories through learned flow-matching.

Consider a robot clearing a cluttered shelf: reaching for a target mug, its arm trajectory passes within millimeters of nearby glasses---a collision invisible to the action policy. Its flow-matching velocity field encodes no notion of obstacle proximity. Yet a critical gap separates laboratory success from real-world deployment: collision safety. When a robot executes a manipulation task in a cluttered environment, unintended contact with surrounding objects or the workspace boundary can damage both the robot and the environment, and create safety hazards for nearby humans. The problem is especially acute for VLA models, which generate entire action chunks in a single forward pass without explicit geometric reasoning about obstacle proximity.

