We draw on an observation from image generation: guidance distillation \citep{meng2023distillation} has shown that inference-time guidance signals can be internalized into model weights during training. We transfer this principle to VLA safety, internalizing SDF collision geometry into flow-matching velocity fields for safe action generation. At low noise levels ($t$ near 0), the model's internal representations encode meaningful geometric structure; an SDF auxiliary head attached to these representations provides a differentiable collision proximity signal. By penalizing collision-prone predictions with a noise-dependent weight that activates only in the low-noise regime, we shape the learned velocity field to route around obstacles without corrupting the training signal at high noise levels.

We propose \ours{}, a training-time method that embeds collision awareness into $\pi_{0.5}$ through SDF auxiliary supervision and a noise-aware collision-shaping loss. A lightweight two-layer network predicts per-timestep minimum signed distances from the action expert's internal features. The collision-shaping loss penalizes trajectories violating a safety margin $\delta$, weighted by $w(t) = \sigma(\alpha(t_0 - t))$ that activates only in the low-noise regime. Both SDF regression and collision penalty gradients propagate through the shared backbone, directly modifying the velocity field. At inference, the SDF head is discarded entirely: the model runs through the standard $\pi_{0.5}$ pipeline with zero runtime overhead. This ``train-with, infer-without'' principle transfers guidance distillation from image generation to safety internalization in robot control.

